1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,290
Listen to a talk in an economics class.

2
00:00:02,290 --> 00:00:07,210
Professor: Today, we are going to talk about the concept of the gig economy.

3
00:00:07,210 --> 00:00:14,438
The gig economy is a labor market characterized by short-term contracts or freelance work,

4
00:00:14,438 --> 00:00:17,020
as opposed to permanent jobs.

5
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:22,595
It has gained significant traction in recent years because of technological advancements

6
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:28,170
and the rise of digital platforms like ride-sharing apps and freelance websites.

7
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:33,890
In a gig economy, workers typically take on multiple short-term jobs or projects,

8
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:40,050
and they often do so through online platforms that connect them directly with clients.

9
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:43,868
This can offer greater flexibility compared to traditional employment,

10
00:00:43,868 --> 00:00:48,110
as workers can choose when and where they wanna work.

11
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:53,641
Companies benefit from the gig economy by reducing overhead costs associated

12
00:00:53,641 --> 00:00:57,160
with full-time employees, like for office space.

13
00:00:57,160 --> 00:01:01,296
This allows them to vary their workforce according to demand,

14
00:01:01,296 --> 00:01:06,260
which can be particularly useful during peak seasons or for specific projects.

15
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:11,059
On the other hand, gig workers may face difficulties in securing consistent work

16
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:15,120
and may have to juggle multiple gigs to make a living.

17
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:21,478
Next, we will discuss potential regulatory responses to protect gig workers and after that:

18
00:01:21,478 --> 00:01:26,020
the implications of the gig economy on traditional labor markets.
